China's spies have got a lot of unwanted  attention recently thanks in part to  political and trade tensions between  China and the US many of those spies  work for the country's main intelligence  agency the Ministry of State Security or  MSS in December 2018 one of China's top  tech executives was arrested in Canada  on a US extradition request  soon after MSS agents abruptly detained  two Canadians in China then days later  the u.s. publicly named two of MSS as  alleged assets as part of a sweeping  u.s. indictment involving hacking on a  global scale  the series of events has people asking  what exactly is the MSS this is your  bloomberg quick take on China's elusive  spies compared with the u.s. the MSS is  something of a mash-up it conducts  intelligence operations abroad like the  CIA counter espionage at home like the  FBI and cyber snooping like the NSA when  you hear the details of how the MSS  operates you think it wouldn't be out of  place in a spy movie the number of  employees is classified and the exact  location of its headquarters isn't  unknown though it's thought to be an  anon descript building in Beijing it  doesn't have a public website and it  doesn't have a press office what's clear  is that the MSS is key to Chinese  President Xi Jinping's efforts to  tighten control over security its head  Chen wen Qing is an ex police chief who  helped direct Xi's nationwide anti-graft  campaign in 2015 the national security  law extended the MSS as power to outer  space and the deep sea as well as the  Internet  the MSS benefits from close ties between  the state and Chinese tech giants like  Baidu and Alibaba and Tencent all of  which have the ability to mine personal  data other government departments are  required to cooperate with the MSS and  provide support for its activities the  MSS operates secret detention facilities  within China and is known for its harsh  treatment of dissidents for example the  blind civil rights lawyer Chen  Guangcheng  was under house arrest for 19 months  before he fled to the US Embassy in  Beijing in 2012 and eventually moved to  New York so the organization has  developed a fearsome reputation and  that's based on how much power it has on  the vague limitations on that power  detainees including foreigners have  alleged violations of their basic rights  while in the custody of security agents  and some have talked about daily  six-hour interrogations not having  access to lawyers or confinement in  cells under lights that never seem to go  out  media reports have noted some key MSS  victories including the possibility that  they monitored president Donald Trump's  calls made on unsecured cellular devices  there was also a report that the MSS had  dismantled CIA operations in China over  several years killing a dozen or more of  the agency's sources reportedly in one  of the worst US intelligence breaches in  decades this isn't an organization  that's completely immune to the global  microscope a senior MSS officer was  arrested in Belgium and extradited to  the US and 2018 on charges of conspiring  to steal trade secrets from top 80 Asian  companies but you have to remember this  is still an organization that's very  powerful that was reportedly the very  first time a spy for China's government  had ever been brought to America to face  charges  [Music]  you
 

